WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.690
My guest this week is the
Oscar-winning American actor,

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.710
producer and world-class
archer, Geena Davis.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:11.040
After shooting to fame in
films such as Beetlejuice,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.300
she's spent a large part

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.390
of her career now
championing gender diversity

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.160
across the media.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.230
I want to ask her today
though about what words

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.510
of advice Dustin Hoffman gave her

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.330
when dealing with tricky male costars

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.270
and how Susan Sarandon changed her life

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.123
when they filmed their iconic
Thelma and Louise movie.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.770
Welcome Geena Davis,
lovely to have you here.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:47.603
Thank you.

00:00:47.603 --> 00:00:50.520
Congratulations on the memoir as well.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.050
And I think there's a theme in there

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.640
that people may be surprised by,

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.520
which is faking it until you make it.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.640
The idea that you as an
actor got your confidence

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.110
as you were going through your career,

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:02.640
not the other way around.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.673
Tell me about that.

00:01:03.673 --> 00:01:08.652
Right, yeah, I was a badass onscreen

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.430
way before I ever felt
like that in real life,

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:17.280
but the roles I played
actually had an impact

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.513
on me personally.

00:01:21.245 --> 00:01:23.820
And I think exactly as you
said, fake it till you make it,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.810
that I was practicing
being somebody confident

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.920
and comfortable in the world.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.270
And it was like an
opportunity to try it on

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.940
and then incorporate it in my real life.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.320
How long do you think it took?

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.750
How old were you when maybe
you felt like a badass

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:43.583
in real life?

00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:48.060
Well, maybe around Thelma
and Louise was the first time

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.900
that I felt like, yeah, I
could be like this person.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.360
Yeah, if you go on a
crime spree with a woman

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.470
on a road trip.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.623
Exactly, I haven't really
gone on any crime sprees yet,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.700
but it's there, the potential is there.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.950
We will definitely come
back to Thelma and Louise,

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.564
but that was in your mid-30s.

00:02:06.564 --> 00:02:08.592
so maybe around that point.
Yes, mid-30s.

00:02:08.592 --> 00:02:11.520
The book is called 'Dying
of Politeness' and as part

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.520
a dedication to your
parents who I was fascinated

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.810
even changed the spelling of
your name to avoid awkwardness.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.780
Right, well, it wasn't
that they changed it.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.540
My real name, or my
given name was Virginia.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.490
And since my aunt was Ginny,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.410
they didn't wanna have the same nickname.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.860
So my three-year-old brother
actually came up with Geena.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:35.400
I don't know how he spelled
it when he said it out loud,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.400
but my mom always made it G-E-E-N-A.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.487
And later, she said, "People seem to love

00:02:42.487 --> 00:02:43.710
"the way your name is spelled."

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.390
I said, yeah, I just tell
them you didn't know how

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:46.560
to spell Gina.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.607
And she said, "Oh, no, no,

00:02:48.607 --> 00:02:50.460
"I grew up in an Italian neighborhood.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.110
I know exactly how to spell it."

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.457
Well, why did you spell it with two Es?

00:02:54.457 --> 00:02:57.947
"Well, I didn't want anyone
to think it was 'gina."

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.970
Like vagina?
Yeah, as in va.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:07.530
And it was like, oh my God,
my whole identity is based

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.650
on fear of vagina.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.140
And fear of stepping out of line

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.820
or drawing too much attention,

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.490
which is a big part of what
you talk about growing up.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.170
Absolutely, absolutely.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.740
And yet, you went on to be an actor,

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.260
to be the center of attention.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.830
See, that's the weird thing
was that from minute one,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.780
I wanted to be an actor from
when I was three or something,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.290
which is to invite people to look at you,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.870
but I didn't want anyone to look at me.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.930
I wanted to be smaller
and take up less space,

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.930
but maybe, this is only in hindsight,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:46.920
maybe I realized if I could
pretend to be somebody else,

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.920
I could be more, have a fuller life

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.690
if I could pretend to be someone else.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.310
Because you started with modeling as well,

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.110
which is the ultimate look at me.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.240
Right, right, exactly.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.700
Just about unwanted looking, though.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.770
It's interesting, a lot of your writing,

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.530
a lot of your work is about
women, but it's the advice

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.980
from a male co-star quite
early on, Dustin Hoffman,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.980
he gives you a line if you are
gonna have unwanted advances

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:17.280
from male costars.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:18.420
Tell us about that.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.240
Yes, he was giving me advice every day

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.187
when I was working with him and he said,

00:04:24.187 --> 00:04:25.747
"Never sleep with your costars."

00:04:25.747 --> 00:04:27.847
"It's just a bad idea, it's not good.

00:04:27.847 --> 00:04:30.120
"So here's what you say."

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.987
And he told me to say, "Thank
you, you're very attractive,

00:04:34.987 --> 00:04:37.807
"I would love to, but I don't wanna ruin

00:04:37.807 --> 00:04:39.987
"the sexual tension between us."

00:04:41.917 --> 00:04:45.690
And then I had to use it on
somebody who was Jack Nicholson.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:46.896
Yeah, not just anyone,
it was Jack Nicholson.

00:04:46.896 --> 00:04:49.980
Yeah, it was Jack Nicholson. I was still-

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:53.940
I had done Tootsie, but
I was also still a model.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:58.940
And my modeling agent took me
and a couple of other models

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.230
who were also actresses
or wanted to be actresses

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.570
to LA and he knew Jack
Nicholson for some reason.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.990
And so every night, we had
dinner with Jack Nicholson

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:10.950
for this week.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.560
And then one time I got
back home to the hotel

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.620
and there was a message, Geena,
please call Jack Nicholson.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.954
I was like, call Jack Nicholson?

00:05:18.954 --> 00:05:21.270
Okay, and I, "Hello, Mr. Nicholson."

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:23.430
"This is Geena, the model."

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:28.227
And he says, "Hey, Geena,
when's it gonna happen?"

00:05:29.851 --> 00:05:32.040
I was like, why didn't I realize

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.903
this was gonna be what it was about?

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.510
But then I was like, I know what to say.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:44.510
So I said, well, Jack,
you know, I have a feeling

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.100
that we're gonna be
working together someday

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.290
and I would hate to have ruined

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.003
the sexual tension between us.

00:05:52.454 --> 00:05:56.040
And he was like, "Oh man,
where'd you get that from?"

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.150
But it worked.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:58.560
Did you say Dustin Hoffman?

00:05:58.560 --> 00:05:59.820
Or you kept it to yourself?
No, I didn't, I didn't.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.940
Okay, because what's
interesting about that line

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.450
and in the book, you are
exploring your responses

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.030
and criticizing yourself as well at times.

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:12.120
You call yourself a wimp, you
say all these sorts of things

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.690
and of course it's the power
of hindsight and experience.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.780
But that particular line Dustin
Hoffman gave you is still

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.580
about flattering the male ego.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.610
Yes, exactly because it included,

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:26.370
I would love to, you're
very attractive, right.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.710
So would you still use that sort of line

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:29.700
or what do you make of that?

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:30.930
Has it stood the test of time?

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:34.710
Well, no, I wouldn't need that line now.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.723
Now I'd just like, nah, whatever.

00:06:37.814 --> 00:06:39.990
But I suppose it shows
you the precariousness

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:44.460
of the women's role in
these working environments.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.150
Right, right, yeah, that he
felt it would be necessary

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.940
for me to boost their
ego at the same time,

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:54.030
instead of saying, no,
I'm not interested in you.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.990
Yes, well, one of the men
that you also write about

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:02.010
is Bill Murray and you
talk about working with him

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.630
and you describe...

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.730
I thought I'd let you
describe it, an incident

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:08.040
that goes on against your consent.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:09.750
How would you describe what happened?

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:12.810
Right, well, it was inappropriate

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:16.440
from minute one of meeting him.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:21.440
I had gone to a hotel to meet
with him, he was the director

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:22.800
of this movie also.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.830
And there as a co-director
there and a producer,

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.080
so it wasn't a booby trap or something

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.970
where I was gonna be alone
with him in a hotel suite.

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:33.457
But as soon as I walked in, he said,

00:07:33.457 --> 00:07:37.260
"Have you ever tried the
thumper," which was some kind

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:41.100
of massage device, a huge massage device.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.197
And I was like, no, no and
went to sit down and he said,

00:07:44.197 --> 00:07:45.575
"No, no, you have to try it.

00:07:45.575 --> 00:07:46.467
"You have to try it, try it.

00:07:46.467 --> 00:07:47.700
"You have to lay on the bed and try it."

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:49.260
And I was like, no, no.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.660
And I tried my hardest to be polite

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:56.520
and certainly I'm not gonna do that.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.190
And then he was so relentless

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:01.890
that I realized I'm not
gonna get out of here

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.200
until I do this.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.515
And so I perched partway on the bed

00:08:06.515 --> 00:08:11.490
and he put it on my back, but that was--

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.500
that was very questionable.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:15.360
You know what that was about.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.790
And I mean, would you describe that...

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.740
How would you describe that?

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:22.110
Well, it turned out later that I found out

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:27.110
that he was worried I was
gonna think that I was all that

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:32.780
because I had just won an
Oscar, and that I would be

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:36.330
on the set and think that
I'm superior to people,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.580
I don't know what he thought.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:42.990
But that was to see if he
could make me do something

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:44.700
I didn't wanna do.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:48.480
Okay, which is a real power
play and very uncomfortable.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.670
It's a huge power play, yeah.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:52.560
But the reason I ask is because

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.620
of the way we describe things
now perhaps has changed again

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:57.540
when you look back on things.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.240
And I wonder why perhaps
you don't describe it

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.410
in stronger terms.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:04.930
Well, right, because even I'm realizing

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.820
after the book was done and everything

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:12.870
that when I describe
it, instead of talking

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.000
about how inappropriate his behavior was,

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:18.450
I'm talking about, I was so sad to see

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.000
that I wasn't the kind of person yet

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.253
who could prevent that from happening,

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:28.857
instead of, really it's
all about his behavior.

00:09:28.857 --> 00:09:31.726
But I suppose there's
still a restraint to you

00:09:31.726 --> 00:09:32.730
when you're doing this.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:36.780
I'm still dying of politeness
or maybe slightly in danger

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:38.219
of dying of politeness.

00:09:38.219 --> 00:09:41.100
But of course, you've taken
work with your institute

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.700
where you look at bias, you
look at behavior and yet,

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:47.460
it's interesting you can't
quite bring it about yourself

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:49.470
and about these sorts of high profile men

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:52.237
that you're willing to
name and talk about.

00:09:52.237 --> 00:09:55.830
Right, yeah, I wonder if
that has something to do

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:59.220
with why I was so interested
in women's empowerment

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:03.820
and everything is that I felt not powerful

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:09.780
and I wanted to try to help
myself as well as others.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:12.270
I think Bill Murray's
been in the news again

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.680
in only the last few days.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:16.440
It's been alleged he settled,
had a financial settlement

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:18.030
with a female production assistant

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.010
over allegedly straddling this woman,

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:21.960
kissing over masks on set.

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:23.730
He said there's a difference of opinion,

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.340
but shooting was stopped
on the production.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:27.840
And we're in 2022.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.463
What's your response to that?

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.000
That he said it was a
difference of opinion?

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:34.380
Yeah, and the whole story.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.470
Yeah, yeah, it's bad, it's bad.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.930
It's utterly inappropriate.

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:43.860
I mean, that anyone could think
that that was a difference

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.080
of opinion when she was
forced to do something

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.613
that she didn't want to do,

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.290
that's not the definition
of what happened.

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:54.450
But I suppose hearing
that story and about...

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.280
I mean, it's alleged
I'll say at this point

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:57.450
and I know you don't know the details,

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:58.320
I don't know the details,

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.160
but all this time on, how
does that make you feel

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:04.110
when you've just described
something that happened to you

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:07.410
at the end of the '80s with the same guy?

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:10.350
Well, look, yeah, I wasn't
under the impression

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:13.620
that he would've changed necessarily.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.297
But I suppose the world has changed.

00:11:15.297 --> 00:11:16.890
The world has changed.

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:19.440
There was a moment of change
you thought, you hoped

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:20.550
after Thelma and Louise.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:21.383
Yes.

00:11:21.383 --> 00:11:23.190
And you actually say in the book,

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:25.647
nothing changed afterwards.

00:11:25.647 --> 00:11:27.570
But you say everything
in your life changed

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:30.090
when you met Susan
Sarandon, professionally so

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:33.963
and also had a big impact
personally for you.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:37.833
What did she have that you
didn't have at that time?

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:44.103
An ease and self-confidence
in any situation,

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:46.260
she knew what she thought about things,

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:47.730
she knew how to react in the moment.

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:52.050
I mean I was living in a
world where hours or minutes

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.660
or days later, I would
realize what I could've said

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:56.670
or should've said or should've done.

00:11:56.670 --> 00:12:01.670
And she just lived in the
moment and was very comfortable

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:03.630
sharing what she thought.

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:05.730
I mean, I'm not saying that
she was confrontational,

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:10.050
but she just had an immediate ease

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:14.463
and knew how she felt about
things when they happened.

00:12:15.861 --> 00:12:20.130
And I came to realize that
I didn't even know how I...

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:22.740
Forget about that I didn't
say what I was thinking,

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:24.780
I didn't even know how
I felt about situations

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:27.120
until I had time to think about it.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:30.030
But from minute one, I was like,

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:32.880
wow, I never met anybody like you before.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.040
I do love the fact that
women come up to you still

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:39.030
and say, "It's our favorite
movie, we've acted it out."

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.441
You probably get worried about that.

00:12:41.441 --> 00:12:44.550
I know, I'm like, hmm,
I don't think you did.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.580
I mean, at least, what part do
you think you're acting out?

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:51.900
But I think we went on
a weekend, just us girls

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:54.090
or something like that, but if you think

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:55.590
about the stuff we do...

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:59.880
You know, people think of
Thelma and Louise as examples

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:02.280
or maybe a little bit
role models slightly,

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:06.300
but nothing we do should be emulated

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:09.240
except being in charge of our own fate.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:10.073
That was the thing.

00:13:10.073 --> 00:13:13.140
That was the whole driving
force in it, but the rest,

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.290
the details, the crime elements...

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.690
The rest was kind of criminal, yeah.

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:21.840
Have you ever been on a
real-life road trip with Susan?

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:25.860
No, no, but I was thinking only recently

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:29.610
that that would be a cool reality show

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:31.541
is Susan and I driving around the country.

00:13:31.541 --> 00:13:32.374
Think we'll have a sequel?

00:13:32.374 --> 00:13:35.130
Everyone who asks you about
that, I know you get very...

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:37.710
I'm not asking that, but the
idea of you two together.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:39.540
Right, right, driving around.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:41.220
Have you asked, you should ask her?

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:43.470
I should ask her, I know!

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:44.970
I mean we would love it.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:46.520
Is she still in your
life, are you friends?

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:49.177
Oh yeah, we're still
very good friends, yeah.

00:13:49.177 --> 00:13:52.110
She's fantastic to be with.

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:54.367
She would often say when we were shooting,

00:13:54.367 --> 00:13:56.460
"See how much fun it
is to work with women?"

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:58.890
And I was like, yes.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:01.740
You talk about the roles
drying up as you got older.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.720
You never thought it would happen to you.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:05.550
I like the way you
describe it, it's awful,

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:07.413
but you say forced retirement.

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:10.470
I know that you're very polite,

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:13.320
but are you angry about that?

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:16.260
It made me angry and very sad,

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:20.100
but sometimes you replace
anger with sadness.

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.699
Yeah, but if you were to
really let it out, Geena,

00:14:22.699 --> 00:14:24.720
in this studio and you know-

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:26.070
it's not a laughing matter it's-

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:29.770
No, no, it wasn't and I'd
heard about this phenomenon

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.960
from the very beginning
that roles drop off

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:37.500
for women once they reach 40,
but I thought Meryl Streep

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:40.740
and all these other female
actors were gonna fix it.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:44.040
And even if it's still true,
that won't happen to me

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:47.520
because I'm in League of
Their Own and all that stuff.

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:52.520
So it was stunning to
see it also happen to me.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:57.240
Yeah, and then I talked about
the Geena Davis Institute

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:00.390
on Gender in the Media looking
at female representation.

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:02.818
I know you've set things up
like the Bentonville Film

00:15:02.818 --> 00:15:03.990
Festival to look at this.

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:06.030
What have you learned through that work

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:09.600
that you feel perhaps is
going to help change things?

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:12.870
Right, what I focus on with my institute

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:15.060
is onscreen representations.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:18.210
And I started the whole thing
because I had a daughter

00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:22.590
and started watching
preschool shows with her

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.230
and saw a huge gender imbalance.

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:27.480
So I'm like, wait a
minute, we're training them

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:30.900
from minute one to have
unconscious gender bias.

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:33.900
Girls see there's not many of them there,

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:36.390
boys see we do all the interesting things.

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:41.390
So that really spurred me on
to wanna try to change that.

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:47.180
But most creators, most
writers, most studio head people

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:52.710
with green light power are male

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:57.320
and so, you have to be able to...

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:00.900
In order to achieve what
I wanted to achieve,

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:05.900
you have to get the men in power also

00:16:06.118 --> 00:16:08.730
to see the problem and wanna fix it.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:10.800
And where do you feel we are with that?

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.130
I mean, as we talk, Harvey
Weinstein's back in court,

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:16.440
we've obviously had the Me Too movement,

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:19.380
but do you feel we're in a better place?

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:22.380
Well, as far as children's
entertainment, I can tell you

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:25.923
that in the 18 years since
we started my institute,

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:29.610
we have achieved parity
in the lead characters

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:33.210
in both children's television
and movies made for families.

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:37.650
So that's a huge difference
from when we started,

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:42.090
but progress in other areas
is pretty glacial as far

00:16:42.090 --> 00:16:46.320
as female directors or
editors or cinematographers.

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:48.840
So your mood at the moment about that

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.870
would you say is optimistic,
are you in a pragmatic place,

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:53.430
where would you say you are?

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:55.713
I am an impatient optimist.

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:04.250
I'm always feeling like we can
get there, we can get there,

00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:10.020
but it's in some years,
it's gonna take decades

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:11.760
to reach parity.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:13.350
We can't snap our fingers

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:16.290
and suddenly Congress is half women.

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:17.280
It's gonna take decades.

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:20.250
We've still only seen a
woman in the White House

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:23.610
when it's played on
television or in the films,

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:26.790
not quite ready yet for
whatever the reason.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:28.020
For whatever the reason.

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:31.470
Can I ask, while this forced
retirement's happening,

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:33.600
I know that's when you were
having your children as well,

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.090
but that wasn't the reason as discussed,

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:37.830
you take archery.

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:39.480
I have to ask about this.

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:42.540
It's one of my favorite facts
I did not know about you

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.114
and you're really good at it.

00:17:44.114 --> 00:17:45.030
Yeah.

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.553
Yeah, how did that happen?

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.850
It makes sense if I explain it.

00:17:50.850 --> 00:17:54.120
So I had been very unathletic as a kid.

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:56.880
I was so tall that I was sure
that I must be uncoordinated,

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:58.590
so I didn't wanna try anything.

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:02.340
And then I got cast in
A League of Their Own

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:05.520
to play the best baseball
player anyone's ever seen.

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:08.160
So of course I had to
train and try to learn

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:10.920
and I was kinda good at it.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:14.940
And I was able to pull off
looking like I really know how

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:17.430
to play baseball.

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:19.530
And I did learn it to some extent.

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:22.890
And then I had to learn other
skills for other movies,

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:25.650
sword fighting and horseback
riding and Taekwondo

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:27.000
and different things.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.100
And I was good at all those, too.

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:32.610
So then I thought, I
wonder if in real life,

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:37.610
I would be good at a sport
and what would I choose?

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:40.200
And I happened to see
archery on television

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:42.330
during the '96 Olympics.

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:45.630
And I thought, wow,
archery is so beautiful.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:50.630
It's very dramatic and
maybe I'll be good at that.

00:18:50.667 --> 00:18:54.960
And so found a coach, took it up at 41

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:57.420
and then became utterly obsessed with it.

00:18:57.420 --> 00:19:00.240
I mean, you nearly went and
represented your country?

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:04.290
Well, I was a semi-finalist
in the Olympic trials.

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:05.910
Which is incredible!

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:07.980
I know, the last thing I ever expected was

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:10.530
to find myself in the
Olympic trials in my 40s.

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:14.040
What do you think it was
about the sport and you?

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.920
I think I probably had a bit

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:20.773
of a natural hand-eye
coordination or something,

00:19:20.773 --> 00:19:23.370
but I dove into it.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:24.900
I became utterly obsessed.

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:29.900
I was practicing four hours
a day and I don't know,

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:32.640
I just was very determined.

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.400
Well, also just talking
about later on in your life,

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:38.490
a late diagnosis of ADHD as well,

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:40.050
what did that mean for you?

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:43.710
How did that explain or
unpack some of your world?

00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:47.100
It was such a relief to find out

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:50.760
that I didn't have a
character flaw necessarily

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:54.870
because my entire life, as a kid as well,

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:57.903
I couldn't start things,
I couldn't finish them.

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:02.670
Once we started having
homework that took more

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:07.670
than one night to do, I had no
ability to stretch things out

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.290
and it was very, very difficult.

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:12.180
And I was ashamed of that

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:14.823
that this was something wrong with me.

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:20.610
So for my whole life, it's
something I had to hide

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:23.790
that I can't complete things and all that.

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:28.790
But as soon as I found I
was diagnosed with ADD,

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:33.090
I was like, oh my God,
that explains everything!

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:35.373
It was such an awakening.

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:41.610
And so, okay, so in other
words, I can start feeling like

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:44.940
this is not my fault, this
doesn't mean I'm a bad person,

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:45.773
you know what I mean?

00:20:45.773 --> 00:20:46.717
This is who you are.

00:20:47.889 --> 00:20:49.820
And did you take anything for it

00:20:49.820 --> 00:20:51.976
or did it change your approach?

00:20:51.976 --> 00:20:55.770
I didn't end up taking
any medication for it,

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:58.110
but it did change my approach.

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:03.110
It made me realize that
I needed coping skills

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:07.110
that would help deal with that.

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:08.670
So for example,

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:12.570
if I felt like I wasn't
gonna practice archery

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:14.820
on a certain day which I wanted to,

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:17.370
but am I gonna make myself get in the car?

00:21:17.370 --> 00:21:18.840
I would make an appointment

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:21.090
for a brief time with
my coach in the morning.

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:24.390
And then once I'm there, I
could stay all day and practice,

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:27.000
but little tricks like that.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:30.570
Would you like to do a lot
more acting again, though?

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:32.370
Oh yeah, absolutely, absolutely.

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:34.230
That's my thing, acting is my thing.

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:36.210
That's what I wanna do.

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:37.440
Yeah, well, at the same time,

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:38.790
you've obviously kept yourself very busy.

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:41.010
But I was thinking about
to, we had Gloria Allred

00:21:41.010 --> 00:21:42.630
on the program, the feminist lawyer

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:45.750
and she was talking about
women getting more radical

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:47.100
as they get older.

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:49.020
Do you feel that has happened to you?

00:21:49.020 --> 00:21:52.590
Well, so I worked with Polly Bergen once,

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:54.303
who was a famous actress.

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:57.750
When I worked with her, she was 80,

00:21:57.750 --> 00:22:00.180
but she had been quite famous

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:05.180
and I couldn't believe
how salty she was on set.

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:08.400
She was swearing here and there, you know?

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:11.370
And I was like, wow, Polly, you really...

00:22:11.370 --> 00:22:13.830
Yeah, you're kinda like a sailor.

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:17.377
And she said, "Oh, when
you get to be my age,

00:22:17.377 --> 00:22:19.080
"you just don't give a anymore."

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:22.680
And I was like, okay,
then I'm gonna be like you

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:23.700
when I get to be your age.

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:24.879
This is where I'm heading for.

00:22:24.879 --> 00:22:25.712
Yeah.

00:22:25.712 --> 00:22:27.420
And are there any dream roles for you

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:29.550
that you think about?

00:22:29.550 --> 00:22:32.400
There's one real-life character
that I would like to play,

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:34.620
who is Eleanor Roosevelt.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:36.730
And the great thing about that is

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:39.270
she was famous her whole life,

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:42.960
so I'm not gonna age out
of being able to play her.

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:44.850
And she was very tall like me.

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:46.740
I think I could look very much like her.

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.900
Yeah, well, there's a great
quote that's always attributed

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.690
to her about a woman being like a tea bag.

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:52.653
You don't know how strong she is

00:22:52.653 --> 00:22:54.780
till you put her in hot water.

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:56.580
I don't know if it is definitely her,

00:22:56.580 --> 00:22:58.410
but I got given it on a pin cushion.

00:22:58.410 --> 00:23:00.180
Oh, I love that.

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:01.110
That's a great quote.

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:02.850
So okay, we've sorted
it, Eleanor Roosevelt

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:05.130
and you're gonna do a
reality TV show with Susan.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:06.450
Sure, yeah.

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:08.160
Well, it's lovely to talk to you

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:10.620
and hear some of your memories
you've shared in the book,

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:13.080
but also where you think
we're at, at the moment.

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:14.430
Geena Davis, thank you so much.

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:15.900
Thank you.

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:18.150
And thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:21.303
Until we meet again,
take care and goodbye.

